        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "1. The horizontal axis inscription identifies left-to-right position as Advertising Spend ($ thousands). 2. The vertical axis inscription identifies bottom-to-top position as Sales ($ thousands). 3. Both printed tick sequences increase in those directions. 4. The axes accurately encode comparable regional values. 5. Labels attach to their adjacent markers. 6. The displayed top regions are sufficiently representative of regions beyond A–E. 7. Association is accepted as sufficient decision evidence rather than proof of advertising causality.",
        "evidence": "Conditions 1-3 are supported by the axis labels and tick marks. Condition 4 is supported as the chart is a standard scatter plot with consistent units. Condition 5 is supported by the visual proximity of labels to points. Condition 6 is open: the chart is titled 'Top Regions' and only shows 5 regions, but there is no public evidence that these 5 are representative of the entire regional population. Condition 7 is open: there is no public policy stating that association is sufficient; the task simply asks to review and submit a decision. Because the representativeness and sufficiency of association are not established, the condition is not fully met.",
